{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>משיתהו.</b> I drew him out — this is an Aramaic expression of drawing out, 'כְּמִשְׁחַל בִּינְתָא מֵחֲלָבָא.' And in Hebrew, <b>משיתיהו</b> is an expression of 'I removed him,' as in לֹא יָמוּשׁ, לֹא מָשׁוּ; so Menachem explained. But I say it is not from the combination of מָשׁ and יָמוּשׁ but from the root מָשָׁה, and it is an expression of drawing out — and so too 'יַמְשֵׁנִי מִמַּיִם רַבִּים' (II Samuel 22). For if it were from the combination of מָשׁ, it would not be proper to say מְשִׁיתִיהוּ but rather הֲמִישׁוֹתִיהוּ — as one says from קָם הֲקִימוֹתִי, and from שָׁב הֲשִׁיבוֹתִי, and from בָּא הֲבִיאוֹתִי, or מַשְׁתִּיהוּ, as in 'וּמַשְׁתִּי אֶת עֲוֹן הָאָרֶץ' (Zechariah 3). But מָשִׁיתִי is only from the pattern of a word whose root is founded on a final heh — such as מָשָׁה, בָּנָה, עָשָׂה, צִוָּה, פָּנָה. When one comes to say in them 'I acted,' the yod comes in place of the heh — as in בָּנִיתִי, עָשִׂיתִי, צִוִּיתִי."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:50:19.804859813Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}